{
  "query": "How can I verify that the rules for each project are fixed before I join?",
  "summary": "Confirm that a project's rules cannot be changed unilaterally by checking its published contracts and terms, its admin controls, and its governance records, then compare what is promised with what the code can actually do.",
  "verification_steps": [
    {
      "step": 1,
      "action": "Read the rules in the smart contract, not only the marketing page",
      "details": "Find the contract address in official docs, then check the verified source code on Etherscan, Basescan, Snowtrace, or BscScan (or the chain's equivalent explorer). Unverified source code is a major red flag."
    },
    {
      "step": 2,
      "action": "Check whether the contract is upgradeable",
      "details": "Look for proxy patterns such as EIP-1967 proxies, or functions like upgradeTo, upgradeToAndCall, or setImplementation. An upgradeable contract means the rules can change after you join, even if the docs say otherwise. Check the Read/Write Proxy tabs on the explorer to see who controls the implementation."
    },
    {
      "step": 3,
      "action": "Identify admin and owner privileges",
      "details": "Look for owner-only functions that can change fees, mint tokens, pause transfers, blacklist addresses, or alter reward parameters. Use the explorer's Read Contract tab to see the owner address."
    },
    {
      "step": 4,
      "action": "Verify who holds admin keys",
      "details": "If the owner is a single externally owned account (EOA), control is centralized. A Safe multisig with several known signers is better. Confirm whether a timelock (for example, OpenZeppelin TimelockController) sits between the multisig and the contract, which gives users time to react to changes."
    },
    {
      "step": 5,
      "action": "Review audits and their scope",
      "details": "Check reports from firms such as CertiK, Trail of Bits, OpenZeppelin, Hacken, or Sherlock. Confirm the audited commit matches the deployed bytecode and that the audit covers the current version, including any later upgrades."
    },
    {
      "step": 6,
      "action": "Check governance history",
      "details": "If rules can change by vote, review past proposals on Snapshot, Tally, or the project's forum. Check voting power distribution on Dune or DeFiLlama, and whether a small group controls most votes."
    },
    {
      "step": 7,
      "action": "Compare published terms with version history",
      "details": "Read the terms of service and rules documents for clauses that allow unilateral changes, such as 'we may modify these terms at any time.' Use the Wayback Machine or the Git history of the docs repository to see whether past rules were quietly changed."
    },
    {
      "step": 8,
      "action": "Ask for written commitments and check announcements",
      "details": "Look for official changelogs and announcement channels, and confirm they are on the project's verified accounts. Be wary of anyone in Telegram or Discord who claims rules are fixed without any on-chain or documented proof."
    }
  ],
